  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (2.5 x^2 + z^2) / 2, drawn at height 0.5 × loss with contour lines. Without noise, steps overshoot along x once the learning rate is above 0.4 and diverge above 0.8; z never overshoots in this range.",
    "Step = −learning rate × (true slope + noise). Each noise part has spread 2.5·√((64−B)/(63·B)) for batch size B: 2.5 at B=1, exactly 0 at B=64. With noise, single steps can raise the loss; it falls only on average.",
    "Both arrows are drawn at 2.5 × the true step length. Orange: the actual mini-batch step the ball takes. Grey: −learning rate × full-data slope (the noise-free step).",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished), or is marked diverged when the loss passes 5 or the ball leaves the drawn square; the ball and paths are then drawn at the square's edge, while the loss readout keeps the true value.",
    "Noise comes from a fixed seed and the run number. Reset replays run 0's noise; New run uses the next run's noise and keeps the last path as the faded ghost.",
    "Run, Pause and Step once control this run; while running, the ball takes 5 steps per second at normal speed."
  ]
}